{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And the waters were split.</b> Its explanation: after the waters were split, for the splitting of the waters came first, and afterward a strong east wind, in order to dry the dampness of the sea's floor so that it would become dry ground — and this is in the manner of (Exodus 16): ״וירם תולעים ויבאש״ — whose meaning is 'after it became putrid.'" "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> ״ויבקעו המים״ — all the waters in the world were split. And they needed to expound thus from the fact that it did not say 'ויבקעו מימיו' — 'its waters were split.' And further, I shall inform you of the cause of the splitting of the Sea of Reeds, and from there you will understand: for it was a necessary matter that all the waters in the world should be split, and that the nature of all of them should change. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, received that ten miracles were performed at the sea, all of which are learned from this passage.<br>The first: the splitting of the waters, according to the plain sense of the verse: ״ויבקעו המים״.<br>The second: that after they were split they became like a vault, in the shape of a roof stretched and spread out above, with the waters on the right and on the left and above, and the path in the middle — this is what is written: ״והמים להם חומה מימינם ומשמאלם״. Habakkuk came and explained that the path in the middle was like a hole, as it is said (Habakkuk 3): ״נקבת במטיו ראש פרזיו״.<br>The third: that the floor did not remain like the floor of other rivers, which have mud, but rather the sea floor hardened like dry ground — this is what is written: ״הלכו ביבשה״.<br>The fourth: that the path of the Egyptians was wet like mud — this is what is written: ״טבעו בים סוף״. And the word 'טביעה' (sinking) connotes mud, from what is written (Psalms 69): ״טבעתי בין מצולה״, and it is written (Jeremiah 38): ״ויטבע ירמיהו בטיט״. And behold, this punishment was a great miracle for Israel, and therefore it is counted among the ten miracles performed for them. The prophet came and explained (Habakkuk 3): ״חמר מים רבים״.<br>The fifth: that the waters froze — this is what is written: ״קפאו תהומות״, which is from the language of (Job 10): ״וכגבינה תקפיאני״, and they hardened like stones, as it is said: ״ירדו במצולות כמו אבן״ — that is, into the depths that were like stone, upon which the heads are smashed. David came and explained (Psalms 74): ״שברת ראשי תנינים על המים״.<br>The sixth: that they did not freeze as one mass but as many pieces, in the manner of many stones arranged one upon another — this is what is written: ״נצבו כמו נד״ — which is an expression of gathering, like a mound or a heap, where the sheaves are arranged one atop another. So the waters of the sea were gathered, pieces upon pieces, like fragments. David came and explained (ibid.): ״אתה פוררת בעזך ים״.<br>The seventh: that they were made into twelve paths for the twelve tribes, and there is an allusion to this in the song — this is what is written: ״זה אלי ואנוהו״. How does ״זה״ say this? The verse states: ״עזי וזמרת יה״ — in Egypt; ״ויהי לי לישועה״ — at the sea; in it He made for me paths equal to the numerical value of ״זה״ — 'This is my El,' and so David said (Psalms 136): ״לגוזר ים סוף לגזרים״.<br>The eighth: that they froze like glass, and those paths were white and bright as crystal, so that they could see one another as they crossed — this is what is written: ״קפאו תהומות בלב ים״, which encompasses one meaning from the language of (Zechariah 14): ״יקרות וקפאון״ — which is a matter of light and something that shines. David, peace be upon him, came and explained (II Samuel 22): ״חשרת מים עבי שחקים באר כי קבוץ המים היה בהיר כעבי שחקים כעצם השמים לטוהר״.<br>The ninth: that He brought forth sweet waters for them from within salty ones — this is what is written: ״נוזלים״, which connotes sweet waters, as it is said (Song of Songs 4): ״באר מים חיים ונוזלים מן לבנון״.<br>The tenth: that they froze and hardened after the sweet flowed, and after they took from them what they drank — so that before they had descended to the earth they were freezing in the heart of the sea — this is what is written: ״נצבו כמו נד נוזלים קפאו״, for the liquid thing froze in the heart of the sea. Behold, these are the ten miracles that were performed for our fathers at the Sea of Reeds." "<b>And you must</b> know that these three verses each have seventy-two (ע״ב) letters, so that among all of them they are two hundred and sixteen (יר״ו) letters. From their tripling there emerges a Name of seventy-two (ע״ב) triads, each Name with its letters tripled. And this Name of seventy-two is drawn from the fourth sephirah, which is חסד. ״ויסע״ corresponds to חסד, ״ויבא״ corresponds to גבורה, and ״ויט״ corresponds to תפארת." "<b>And you should be roused</b> to understand that the first letters of the first verse, and also the last letters of the third verse, which come out in their order and toward the right side, but the middle ones, which come out of the second verse, which belong to the quality of גבורה, go in reverse order and toward the left side. For it begins from the last ה׳ of the night. And behold, all three of them grasp one another, and from them one Name of seventy-two is made, and all its letters are bound and interwoven with the three patriarchs of the supernal chariot." "<b>Know that</b> this sea through which Israel passed was the Sea of Reeds, even though it did not mention this explicitly, not in the command of HaShem and not in the narrative, but only without specification. But in the song that they uttered when they passed through, it mentions ״טבעו בים סוף״, and likewise after they passed it says ״ויסע משה את ישראל מים סוף״ — and it is the last sea, therefore it is called ים סוף. And so David mentioned (Psalms 106:9): ״ויגער בים סוף ויחרב״. However, there is a quality called ״ים״ (sea) through which Israel crossed the sea, and this is ״וימרו על ים״ — and ״בים סוף״ is also called ים סוף, for it is the end of the ascents."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {1 "Comment [1]: in the seventh miracle, the text 'מנין ז״ה' uses a numerical-value abbreviation for זה (=12), meaning the twelve paths; translated hyper-literally preserving the abbreviation's force."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:59:28.831341512Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}